    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Isfahan was a regional centre under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and a city of mixed peoples under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, and it grew under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, whose ruler Toghril Beg made it his capital in the mid-11th century.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s army killed, reportedly, 70,000 of its people in 1387, but in 1598 Abbas the Great made it the Safavid capital and rebuilt it round a new square and a boulevard, settling craftsmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> there by force.</li><li>After Central Asian invaders sacked it in 1722 it declined, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, and in 1775 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s soldiers reportedly killed 70,000 people in Isfahan in 1387, and an eyewitness counted more than 28 towers, each built of about 1,500 heads.</p></aside>
    </section>

