    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">538 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> issues the edict allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and some settle in Isfahan instead</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">642</span><span class="rf-text">Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> capture Isfahan and make it the capital of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> province</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Mid-11th century</span><span class="rf-text">Toghril Beg makes Isfahan the capital of the Seljuk domains</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1387</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s soldiers massacre the people of Isfahan after a revolt against his taxes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text">Abbas the Great moves his capital from Qazvin to Isfahan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1650</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> builds the Khaju Bridge</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1722</span><span class="rf-text">Central Asian invaders sack Isfahan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1775</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a> moves the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_II_(The_Great)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surenae, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Human habitation in the Isfahan region can be traced back to the Palaeolithic period, and archaeologists have found artefacts of the Palaeolithic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and Iron ages. Under Median rule the commercial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> began to show signs of settled urban life and grew into a regional centre, helped by the fertile soil on the banks of the Zayandeh River, in a region called Aspandana or Ispandana.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> joined Persian and Median lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, the city was already religiously and ethnically diverse and became an early example of the king's reputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. In 538 BCE, after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, Cyrus made an edict allowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Babylon to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and some of those who emigrated settled in Isfahan instead. The 10th-century historian Ibn al-Faqih wrote that Jews fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> carried a sample of the water and soil of Jerusalem and settled where the soil and water were thought to resemble it, in a place called Yahudia.</p><p>The name Ispahan comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> Spahān, found on seals and inscriptions including that of the Zoroastrian magus Kartir. Ptolemy gives it as Aspadana or Aspazana, which means 'place of gathering for the army', and the name is believed to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> plural spādānām, 'the armies'. Other ancient names were Gey, Jey, Partakān, Park and Judea. The Zoroastrian city had the Parthian and Achaemenid name Gabae, shortened under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> to Gay, on the northern bank of the Zayandeh.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i.jpg" alt="Malik-Shah I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malik-Shah_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid Al-Din, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Arab capture of 642, Isfahan grew prosperous under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, and its walls are thought to date from the 10th century. Toghril Beg, the Turkish founder of the Seljuk dynasty, made it his capital in the mid-11th century, and it grew in size and splendour under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a>, who reigned from 1073 to 1092. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> reported 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the city in 1170. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> fell, about 1200, Isfahan temporarily declined and was eclipsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and Qazvin. During his visit in 1327 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> noted that it was one of the largest and fairest of cities but in ruins for the greater part.</p><p>The city's second golden age began in 1598, when Abbas the Great moved his capital from Qazvin to the more central Isfahan and rebuilt it as one of the largest cities of the 17th century. He pursued policies to increase Iranian involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade. The plan added the rectangular Shah Square and the straight Chahar Bagh boulevard, with a large garden, now the gardens of Hasht Behesht, between them. A turquoise gate joined the square to the Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">Bazaar</a> and the old square, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mosque">Shah Mosque</a> on the south side became the main place of worship.</p><p>Sheikh Baha'i, an engineer of Abbas, designed a network of canals called madi that carried water from the Zayandeh to different parts of the city: 77 on the northern course of the river and 71 on the southern. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahverdi-khan">Allahverdi Khan</a> Bridge, Si-o-se-pol, 295 metres long, joined the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a>. Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> built the Khaju Bridge in 1650. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin">Jean Chardin</a> wrote that there were 273 baths in Safavid Isfahan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar.jpg" alt="Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_portrait_of_Agha_Muhammad_Khan,_Persia,_Qajar,_dated_1795.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sotheby's, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> the site was a regional centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> ruled it from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, who held power from 247 BCE to 224 CE, Arsacid governors administered the provinces from Isfahan, whose urban development quickened to suit a capital. In the Sasanian era, from 224 to 651 CE, a group called the Espoohrans, descended from seven noble Iranian families, governed the city. Its political importance declined, but its military role grew, and Sasanian princes studied statecraft there. Its position at the crossing of the roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> made it a base for a standing army.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> captured Isfahan in 642 and made it the capital of al-Jabal province, much of ancient Media. The Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>, which came to rule much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as Abbasid authority waned in the 10th century, was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> under Toghril Beg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a>. Isfahan surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1387. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> held power from 1501 to 1736, and Abbas the Great, who reigned from 1588 to 1629, made it his capital in 1598, the second time in its history that it had been the capital of Iran.</p><p>After the Safavids the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharids</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> under the Zand, and in 1775 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>. Early in the 19th century Mohammad Hossein Khan began to preserve some of Isfahan's archaeologically important buildings, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Isfahan&#8221; cites where it covers Median and Achaemenid era, Sassanid era, Timurid era, Safavid era:</p><ul><li>Rudi Matthee, Persia in Crisis: Safavid Decline and the Fall of Isfahan (I.B.Tauris, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-chardin.jpg" alt="Jean Chardin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin">Jean Chardin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Chardin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to John Michael Wright, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Isfahan was home to many peoples. Under Cyrus it was religiously and ethnically diverse, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> tolerance continued. Queen Shushandukht, the Jewish wife of Yazdegerd I (reigned 399 to 420) and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-v">Bahram V</a>, is said to have settled a colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> at Yahudiyyeh, about 3 kilometres from the Zoroastrian city, and after the Arab conquest the population of Gay shrank while that of Yahudiyyeh and its suburbs grew, forming the nucleus of the later city.</p><p>Abbas the Great forcibly resettled Turkish, Armenian and Persian craftsmen in the city to ensure its prosperity. As many as 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julfa-nakhchivan">Jugha</a>, were forcibly resettled, and some were brought to Isfahan, where he ordered a new quarter for them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a>, named from Old Julfa. Most of its churches stand there, among them St Jakob (1607), St Mary (1613), Bedkhem (1627) and Vank Cathedral (1664), and Pacifique de Provins founded a French mission in the city in 1627. The royal court held many Georgian military servants and women, and the city had enclaves of Georgian, Circassian and Daghestani descent, estimated at 20,000 by Engelbert Kaempfer, who was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1684 and 1685.</p><p>Opium dens and coffeehouses around the Chahar Bagh and the Chehel Sotoun served as social hubs, and the Isfahan School of painting flourished in the Safavid era. Isfahan became known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and travellers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin">Jean Chardin</a> described it. The Esfahani dialect is one of the main dialects of Western Persian, and the city's Jewish districts speak a distinct dialect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1387 Isfahan surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and was at first treated with relative mercy. The city then revolted against his punitive taxes, killing the tax collectors and some of his soldiers. In retribution Timur ordered a massacre of the residents, and his soldiers reportedly killed 70,000 citizens. An eyewitness counted more than 28 towers, each built of about 1,500 heads.</p><p>The city had already declined after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> about 1200, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> saw much of it in ruins in 1327. In 1722, during a marked decline in Safavid influence, Central Asian invaders sacked Isfahan. Afterwards the city lost importance, and the capital moved away, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>.</p><p>In the 20th century people from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> settled in Isfahan, many during the migrations early in the century and in the 1980s after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>, in which 23,000 people from Isfahan were killed and there were 43,000 veterans. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Polish refugees, mostly children, were admitted to the city, with 21 childcare units and some 2,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> housed there in February 1943, before they were evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square.jpg" alt="Naqsh-e Jahan Square" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naqsh-i_Jahan_Square,_Jan._2018.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">احمد جمالی مقدم, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fame of Isfahan produced the Persian proverb Esfahān nesf-e-jahān ast, 'Isfahan is half the world'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a>, also called Shah Square or Imam Square, is among the largest city squares in the world and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The city centre has an older section round the Jameh Mosque and the Safavid expansion round the square, with palaces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and places of worship nearby, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu">Ali Qapu</a>, Chehel Sotoun and Hasht Behesht palaces. The Grand Bazaar and its Qeysarie Gate were built in the 17th century.</p><p>Isfahan is known for the Isfahan rug, usually of merino wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and the city still produces carpets, textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, handicrafts and traditional sweets. It also keeps ancient festivals such as Tirgan and Sepandārmazgān, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> performances began there in 1919. The Armenian quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a> is today one of the largest Armenian quarters in the world.</p><p>The city is the capital of Isfahan Province and the third most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, with approximately 2,238,000 people, and lies 440 kilometres south of Tehran. It stands where the two principal routes across Iran, north to south and east to west, meet.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Isfahan, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, was a regional centre under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and a capital under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and, from 1598, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, when Abbas the Great rebuilt it round a new square and boulevard. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s massacre of 1387 and a sack in 1722 marked its setbacks, and it remains one of Iran's largest cities.</em></p>

